555fdfd161f2506","message":"继续执行，不用等我。\n请做 maintainer 视角复核。\n\n交付：\n1）P1 diagnostics gap issue 的 5 个风险表述（容易被打回）\n2）每个风险表述的 safer rewrite\n3）maintainer objection list（至少 5 条）+ 对应简短应答\n4）结论：P1 先发 issue，还是先发 docs PR，给明确判断\n\n按“状态/任务/结果/原因/建议”直接回 main。","timeoutSeconds":5},"partialJson":"{\"sessionKey\":\"agent:aoao:feishu:aoao:direct:ou_0dee72e5f9e6a83b0555fdfd161f2506\",\"message\":\"继续执行，不用等我。\\n请做 maintainer 视角复核。\\n\\n交付：\\n1）P1 diagnostics gap issue 的 5 个风险表述（容易被打回）\\n2）每个风险表述的 safer rewrite\\n3）maintainer objection list（至少 5 条）+ 对应简短应答\\n4）结论：P1 先发 issue，还是先发 docs PR，给明确判断\\n\\n按“状态/任务/结果/原因/建议”直接回 main。\",\"timeoutSeconds\":5}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":7357,"output":661,"cacheRead":13824,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":21842,"cost":{"input":0.018392500000000003,"output":0.009915,"cacheRead":0.003456,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0317635}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774413565821}}
{"type":"message","id":"a5ee3779","parentId":"3904dfb9","timestamp":"2026-03-25T04:39:44.163Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_RNnddupW1q4dWadBGwT3Cj5u|fc_0d8886978b870ccc0169c3670ad63c81919b71d1ad39eed9c4","toolName":"sessions_send","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"runId\": \"12b3d052-2b1d-4ac1-bc73-f011f4e1a2d9\",\n  \"status\": \"timeout\",\n  \"sessionKey\": \"agent:xixi:feishu:xixi:direct:ou_7e95c6c474ffd4086e3a05454dd29c24\"\n}"}],"details":{"runId":"12b3d052-2b1d-4ac1-bc73-f011f4e1a2d9","status":"timeout","sessionKey":"agent:xixi:feishu:xixi:direct:ou_7e95c6c474ffd4086e3a05454dd29c24"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1774413584156}}
{"type":"message","id":"eaea9e5d","parentId":"a5ee3779","timestamp":"2026-03-25T04:39:49.260Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_KfWRzbN6p64SFupdQnKxIabr|fc_0d8886978b870ccc0169c3670ad65081919dad4f81db464526","toolName":"sessions_send","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"runId\": \"31633fbd-c4d2-48ce-b88c-43aec6a22960\",\n  \"status\": \"timeout\",\n  \"sessionKey\": \"agent:aoao:feishu:aoao:direct:ou_0dee72e5f9e6a83b0555fdfd161f2506\"\n}"}],"details":{"runId":"31633fbd-c4d2-48ce-b88c-43aec6a22960","status":"timeout","sessionKey":"agent:aoao:feishu:aoao:direct:ou_0dee72e5f9e6a83b0555fdfd161f2506"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1774413589252}}
{"type":"message","id":"7e5a0716","parentId":"eaea9e5d","timestamp":"2026-03-25T04:40:07.354Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_gXM746ti10htTnPoVBAYkdq2|fc_0d8886978b870ccc0169c367164cf481918dfbe02b6786688d","name":"write","arguments":{"file_path":"/root/.openclaw/workspace/project/openclaw-contrib-2026-03-25.md","content":"# OpenClaw Contribution Push — 2026-03-25\n\n## Current strategy\n\nDo not frame the Feishu group routing behavior as a confirmed bug.\n\nPreferred framing:\n- diagnostics gap\n- docs gap\n- observability gap\n\nCore point:\nDevelopers currently lack enough visibility to tell whether inbound routing matched an explicit binding or fell back to the default agent. Even if the runtime behavior is intentional, the missing diagnostics/docs make the behavior hard to reason about and easy to misinterpret.\n\n## P1 — Diagnostics gap issue\n\n### Candidate titles\n1. Expose whether inbound routing matched a binding or fell back to the default agent\n2. Improve routing diagnostics for inbound messages that resolve via fallback\n3. Clarify routing decisions in diagnostics when bindings are not explicitly matched\n\n### Draft issue body (v1)\n\n#### Background\nWhen testing inbound routing in Feishu group conversations, it can be difficult to determine why a message was delivered to a specific agent/session. In particular, there are cases where a group message appears to reach an agent even though there is no obvious explicit group binding visible from the operator's perspective.\n\n#### What is unclear\nAt the moment, it is hard to tell whether the routing decision came from:\n- an explicit binding match,\n- canonical session-key resolution,\n- or fallback to a default agent/path.\n\nBecause this decision is not surfaced clearly in diagnostics, operators can easily misread expected fallback behavior as a binding bug.\n\n#### Why this matters\nThis creates two problems:\n1. It slows down debugging when configuring multi-agent routing.\n2. It makes documentation harder to trust, because users cannot map the observed runtime behavior back to a clear routing explanation.\n\nEven if the current behavior is working as designed, the lack of observability makes it difficult to verify that design.\n\n#### Suggested improvement\nIt would help if OpenClaw exposed a more explicit routing explanation in diagnostics/logs/status output, for example indicating whether an inbound message:\n- matched an explicit binding,\n- resolved via a canonical session/group mapping,\n- or fell back to a default agent.\n\nThis would make routing behavior much easier to debug without presuming the current implementation is incorrect.\n\n#### Minimal reproduction (abstract)\n1. Configure Feishu with a default agent and at least one explicit binding pattern.\n2. Send a message from a group conversation that is not obviously covered by the operator's expected binding rules.\n3. Observe that the message is still routed successfully.\n4. Try to determine from current diagnostics/logs whether that happened because of an explicit binding, canonical session resolution, or fallback.\n\nCurrent difficulty: the operator can observe the result, but not the routing reason with enough confidence.\n\n## P2 — Docs PR outline\n\n### Goal\nExplain how group routing decisions relate to canonical group session keys and fallback/default-agent behavior.\n\n### Minimal doc changes\n1. Routing/bindings docs\n   - Add one paragraph explaining that successful delivery does not always imply an explicit binding match.\n   - State that canonical session resolution or default-agent fallback may also explain the outcome, depending on configuration.\n\n2. Feishu/group-chat docs\n   - Add one short example showing a group message that lands in the expected agent session even when the operator assumes a binding should have been required.\n   - Explain what to check when diagnosing that result.\n\n3. Diagnostics/troubleshooting docs\n   - Add a checklist: confirm binding, confirm session key shape, confirm fallback/default-agent settings, then compare logs.\n\n### Why this is the smallest mergeable PR\n- No behavior change required\n- Low controversy\n- Converts a confusing runtime observation into explicit operator guidance\n- Complements the diagnostics issue instead of depending on it\n\n## Current recommendation\n- First: open the diagnostics gap issue (P1)\n- Then: prepare the docs PR (P2)\n- If maintainer response suggests behavior is expected, convert that answer into precise docs wording and land P2 quickly\n"},"partialJson":"{\"file_path\":\"/root/.openclaw/workspace/project/openclaw-contrib-2026-03-25.md\",\"content\":\"# OpenClaw Contribution Push — 2026-03-25\\n\\n## Current strategy\\n\\nDo not frame the Feishu group routing behavior as a confirmed bug.\\n\\nPreferred framing:\\n- diagnostics gap\\n- docs gap\\n- observability gap\\n\\nCore point:\\nDevelopers currently lack enough visibility to tell whether inbound routing matched an explicit binding or fell back to the default agent. Even if the runtime behavior is intentional, the missing diagnostics/docs make the behavior hard to reason about and easy to misinterpret.\\n\\n## P1 — Diagnostics gap issue\\n\\n### Candidate titles\\n1. Expose whether inbound routing matched a binding or fell back to the default agent\\n2. Improve routing diagnostics for inbound messages that resolve via fallback\\n3. Clarify routing decisions in diagnostics when bindings are not explicitly matched\\n\\n### Draft issue body (v1)\\n\\n#### Background\\nWhen testing inbound routing in Feishu group conversations, it can be difficult to determine why a message was delivered to a specific agent/session. In particular, there are cases where a group message appears to reach an agent even though there is no obvious explicit group binding visible from the operator's perspective.\\n\\n#### What is unclear\\nAt the moment, it is hard to tell whether the routing decision came from:\\n- an explicit binding match,\\n- canonical session-key resolution,\\n- or fallback to a default agent/path.\\n\\nBecause this decision is not surfaced clearly in diagnostics, operators can easily misread expected fallback behavior as a binding bug.\\n\\n#### Why this matters\\nThis creates two problems:\\n1. It slows down debugging when configuring multi-agent routing.\\n2. It makes documentation harder to trust, because users cannot map the observed runtime behavior back to a clear routing explanation.\\n\\nEven if the current behavior is working as designed, the lack of observability makes it difficult to verify that design.\\n\\n#### Suggested improvement\\nIt would help if OpenClaw exposed a more explicit routing explanation in diagnostics/logs/status output, for example indicating whether an inbound message:\\n- matched an explicit binding,\\n- resolved via a canonical session/group mapping,\\n- or fell back to a default agent.\\n\\nThis would make routing behavior much easier to debug without presuming the current implementation is incorrect.\\n\\n#### Minimal reproduction (abstract)\\n1. Configure Feishu with a default agent and at least one explicit binding pattern.\\n2. Send a message from a group conversation that is not obviously covered by the operator's expected binding rules.\\n3. Observe that the message is still routed successfully.\\n4. Try to determine from current diagnostics/logs whether that happened because of an explicit binding, canonical session resolution, or fallback.\\n\\nCurrent difficulty: the operator can observe the result, but not the routing reason with enough confidence.\\n\\n## P2 — Docs PR outline\\n\\n### Goal\\nExplain how group routing decisions relate to canonical group session keys and fallback/default-agent behavior.\\n\\n### Minimal doc changes\\n1. Routing/bindings docs\\n   - Add one paragraph explaining that successful delivery does not always imply an explicit binding match.\\n   - State that canonical session resolution or default-agent fallback may also explain the outcome, depending on configuration.\\n\\n2. Feishu/group-chat docs\\n   - Add one short example showing a group message that lands in the expected agent session even when the operator assumes a binding should have been required.\\n   - Explain what to check when diagnosing that result.\\n\\n3. Diagnostics/troubleshooting docs\\n   - Add a checklist: confirm binding, confirm session key shape, confirm fallback/default-agent settings, then compare logs.\\n\\n### Why this is the smallest mergeable PR\\n- No behavior change required\\n- Low controversy\\n- Converts a confusing runtime observation into explicit operator guidance\\n- Complements the diagnostics issue instead of depending on it\\n\\n## Current recommendation\\n- First: open the diagnostics gap issue (P1)\\n- Then: prepare the docs PR (P2)\\n- If maintainer response suggests behavior is expected, convert that answer into precise docs wording and land P2 quickly\\n\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":264,"output":888,"cacheRead":21760,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":22912,"cost":{"input":0.0006600000000000001,"output":0.01332,"cacheRead":0.0054399999999999995,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.01942}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774413589258}}
